\title{The Computer Scientist's Approach to Proposition and Proof}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2025-09-05}
\meta{toc}{true}

% The feedback loop for software is different from that of mathematicians because there often isn't a theory with which one can probe, and test their definitions.

\p{
    Some software or computer logic is both highly technical and explicitly mathematical.
    Depending on the level of computation, the goal of a bit of code—maybe something backing a [zero-knowledge proof](dhsorens-001L) or the design of a chip—may be extremely easy to state precisely because the purpose of the software is expressly technical and/or mathematical.
    In these cases, the \em{specification} tends to be abundantly clear because the work of settling on the correct definition and desired behavior of things was solved mathematically before it was translated into software.
}

\p{
    However, software does much more than mathematical computation.
    As it gets higher in abstraction, software can become a social and financial intermediary between people, or even an agent of its own.
    The interface by which we use technology is software;
    the flow of a sales team's CRM is software;
    the media by which news gets reported and propagated is software.
    Software makes music, tells stories, implements art and design;
    it manages money and communication between people.
}

\p{
    At these higher layers of abstraction, software can become difficult to specify \em{correctly}.
    By this I mean that the intended and desired behavior of software can be difficult to articulate in prose or in formal language.
}

\transclude{dhsorens-001M} % AI alignment/specification

\p{
    The specification of LLMs may be intractable, but I think we can make progress in something less, say, human: [smart contracts](dhsorens-000Y).
}

\transclude{dhsorens-001J} % smart contract specification
